---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
import CubbyStory from './CubbyStory.astro';
import ScreenshotSpecimen from '../ScreenshotSpecimen.astro';
import dashboard from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/dashboard.webp';
import dashboardRoutines from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/dashboard-routines.webp';
import dashboardFamily from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/dashboard-family.webp';
import tasks from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/tasks.webp';
import limits from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/limits.webp';
import market from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/market.webp';
import finance from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/finance.webp';
import expiry from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/expiry.webp';
import gymWorkout from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/gym-workout.webp';
import coffee from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/coffee.webp';
import meals from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/meals.webp';
import illness from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/illness.webp';
import travel from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/travel.webp';
import journalRecap from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/journal-recap.webp';
import growingUp from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/growing-up.webp';
import enrichment from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/enrichment.webp';
import screentimer from '$lib/screenshots/cubby/screentimer.webp';
import CubbyLogo from '$lib/assets/cubby-logo.webp';

const groups = [
	{
		label: 'Routines',
		note: 'recurring upkeep, limits, and schedules',
		intro:
			'Once I could check when something was last done, I wanted the same record for the routines we repeated as a family.',
		items: [
			{
				src: tasks,
				alt: 'Cubby tasks',
				headline: 'When did I last do this?',
				paragraphs: [
					"I started with towels because I kept forgetting when I'd last washed them. A dated log was enough for that, but my kid's nasal spray needed more care. The routine changed from daily to every two days, then three, while we also tried to track congestion. Our few Telegram messages left us with very little to tell the doctor.",
					'Each entry now keeps the interval that applied when we logged it. Changing the routine leaves that history intact, so I can look back and see what we were doing at the time.',
				],
			},
			{
				src: limits,
				alt: 'Cubby limits',
				headline: 'On pace, or already over',
				paragraphs: [
					'Screen time kept growing, and I wanted to keep an eye on soda and beer too. I gave each habit a weekly limit and a daily pace, so I could check how the week was going while there was still time to do something about it.',
					"Drinks needed unit conversion because a McDonald's small Coke and a can contain different amounts. Seeing the week's allowance nearly gone has talked me out of a drink more than once.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: screentimer,
				alt: 'Cubby screen timer',
				headline: 'The same timers, by hand, every day',
				paragraphs: [
					'The screen timer gave me a way to connect that weekly limit to what we actually did. YouTube countdowns had fixed durations and sounds, so I built saved profiles with AI voices and ticking sounds that my kid could listen to with his eyes closed.',
					'When a timer finishes, it logs against the limit automatically. That saved a second entry and gave the two features a reason to live in the same app.',
				],
			},
			{
				src: enrichment,
				alt: 'Cubby enrichment schedule',
				headline: 'The schedule nobody could find',
				paragraphs: [
					"Our kid's classes ended at a different time each day. New terms brought new timings, and make-up sessions went into pinned Telegram messages and emails. We mixed them up often enough to spend quite a bit of time waiting at the door.",
					"I put today's schedule on the dashboard, with make-up sessions marked. Pickup time was something we needed every day, so it belonged on the first screen.",
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Health',
		note: 'four bodies, one of them a dog',
		intro:
			'Some records needed more detail than a date. A workout or an illness had to make sense when I came back to it later.',
		items: [
			{
				src: gymWorkout,
				alt: 'Cubby gym workout',
				headline: 'What should I aim for today',
				paragraphs: [
					'At the gym I mostly went by whatever I remembered from the last session. On cable machines, that meant agar agar the position of the pin. I started logging weights, reps, rest times, and notes on failure or dropsets so I could check the previous set before the next one.',
					"There's also a lock screen for sweaty hands around buttons mid-workout. A new personal record posts to the journal, so it shows up alongside the other things we've been doing.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: illness,
				alt: 'Cubby illness log',
				headline: "One log when everyone's sick at once",
				paragraphs: [
					'Keeping a routine history helped, but illness needed a record of its own. Temperatures, doses, medicine timings, and MCs were scattered through Telegram, sometimes for several of us at once. By the consultation, the order of events was hard to reconstruct.',
					"I grouped the entries by illness episode so I could read them out to the doctor. It works for me, my wife, our kid, and the dog. Vet visits can be eight to twelve months apart, so I keep the vet's guidance with the episode and pull it up next time.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: coffee,
				alt: 'Cubby coffee recipes',
				headline: 'Which brew actually tasted better',
				paragraphs: [
					'Coffee was a smaller version of the same memory problem. I could recall the last cup or two, so I started saving bean and water weights, timing, and clicks on my Kingrinder K6. A taste rating lets me compare what I actually liked across bags and roasters.',
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Home',
		note: 'what comes in and what runs out',
		intro: 'The smaller things stayed too, because I kept running into them at home.',
		items: [
			{
				src: meals,
				alt: 'Cubby meals',
				headline: 'What did we eat yesterday?',
				paragraphs: [
					"We were just as bad at remembering meals, sometimes even what we'd had that afternoon. Now I log them at the table before we leave, with a photo, so I can look back at what we've eaten over the week.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: expiry,
				alt: 'Cubby expiry tracker',
				headline: 'The dates that actually matter',
				paragraphs: [
					"A friend mentioned her cosmetics expiring, which made me think about the milk and soy milk in our fridge. Cubby calculates the expiry from when I open something and how long it keeps. I'm more conscious of how long the milk has left now, even if I don't always keep to it.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: market,
				alt: 'Cubby market prices',
				headline: 'Which price is actually worth it',
				paragraphs: [
					"I had a rough sense of supermarket prices, but wine promotions and my Just Wine Club subscription made comparisons harder. I started recording prices by item, shop, and promotion, with unit prices to make different pack sizes comparable. The useful part is knowing which price I'd buy at again.",
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Finance',
		note: 'the dates that cost money',
		intro: 'Remembering a date mattered rather more when missing it cost us money.',
		items: [
			{
				src: finance,
				alt: 'Cubby finance',
				headline: "The fee we couldn't waive",
				paragraphs: [
					"I'd missed credit card bills before and had the charges waived, but my wife's annual fee wasn't waived, and it was expensive. I put card fees, bills, and subscriptions together with their dates so we could check what was coming up.",
				],
			},
		],
	},
	{
		label: 'Life',
		note: 'the things worth keeping',
		intro: 'Once we were using Cubby for the everyday things, I brought in trips and the moments I wanted to keep.',
		items: [
			{
				src: travel,
				alt: 'Cubby travel planner',
				headline: 'Every trip, scattered everywhere',
				paragraphs: [
					"Trips brought a different pile of things to find: emails, Telegram messages, browser tabs, and PDFs, including password-protected insurance documents. I'd already built Roamichi as a travel planner, so I folded it into Cubby. It still needs manual entry and isn't a full itinerary planner, but the trip details are together.",
				],
			},
			{
				src: growingUp,
				alt: 'Cubby growing up',
				headline: 'The moments worth keeping',
				paragraphs: [
					"I'd tried recording things my wife did that I appreciated, along with things that frustrated me. I'd gone down a similar path on The Standup Podcast, and a complaint ledger about your spouse felt toxic.",
					'I turned it into a record for our kid: parent-teacher conferences, teacher feedback, naughty moments, and the sweet ones. Those were things I wanted us to remember.',
				],
			},
		],
	},
];

const contents = [
	{ id: 'notes', label: 'Dashboard', note: 'what belongs here' },
	...groups.map((group) => ({ id: group.label.toLowerCase(), label: group.label, note: group.note })),
	{ id: 'recap', label: 'Recap', note: 'everything rolled up' },
	{ id: 'lessons', label: "What I've learned", note: 'keeping it usable' },
];

const stack = [
	{ role: 'Runtime', tools: 'Go' },
	{ role: 'Frontend', tools: 'SvelteKit, Tailwind' },
	{ role: 'Distribution', tools: 'Progressive Web App' },
	{ role: 'Data', tools: 'PostgreSQL' },
	{ role: 'Auth', tools: 'Stytch' },
	{ role: 'Observability', tools: 'Grafana Cloud, Umami, Uptime Kuma' },
];
---

<div class="cubby-case-study text-base-content bg-base-200">
	<div class="border-neutral/15 bg-base-100 sticky top-0 z-10 border-b">
		<div class="text-base-content/50 mx-auto flex max-w-5xl items-center justify-between px-5 py-2.5 text-xs">
			<a href="/#projects" class="hover:text-base-content flex items-center gap-1.5">
				<Icon name="arrow-left" class="size-[1.2em]" /> Projects
			</a>
			<span>Cubby</span>
		</div>
	</div>

	<header class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pt-12 pb-14 lg:pt-16">
		<div class="grid items-center gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.2fr)_minmax(0,0.8fr)] lg:gap-14">
			<div class="min-w-0">
				<img src={CubbyLogo.src} alt="Cubby" class="h-12" />
				<h1
					class="mt-6 max-w-4xl text-4xl leading-[1.05] font-black tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl">
					It started with my towels and ended up managing our home.
				</h1>
				<p class="text-base-content/80 mt-6 max-w-2xl text-lg leading-relaxed">
					We kept medicine timings and class schedules in Telegram, trip details in emails, and washing routines mostly
					in our heads. I started with a towel reminder and kept adding things we needed. Now a household of four and a
					dog uses Cubby.
				</p>
				<a
					href="https://cubby.dev"
					target="_blank"
					rel="noopener"
					class="cubby-visit hover:bg-base-100 mt-7 inline-flex items-center gap-2 border px-4 py-2 text-sm font-semibold whitespace-nowrap transition-colors focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2">
					Visit site <Icon name="external-link" class="size-[1.1em]" />
				</a>
			</div>
			<div style="view-transition-name: project-hero-cubby;" class="flex justify-center lg:justify-end">
				<div class="border-neutral/15 bg-base-100 w-60 overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border shadow-xl sm:w-68 lg:w-72">
					<Image
						src={dashboard}
						alt="Cubby dashboard screen"
						width={360}
						densities={[1, 2]}
						loading="eager"
						class="block h-auto w-full object-cover"
					/>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</header>

	<CubbyStory section="mockups" />

	<details data-case-study-section-nav class="case-study-contents mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14 sm:hidden">
		<summary
			class="cubby-contents-summary focus-visible:outline-accent text-base-content/80 flex min-h-11 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 border-y py-3 text-xs font-semibold tracking-wide focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2">
			<span>Explore {contents.length} sections</span>
			<Icon name="chevron-right" data-motion="spatial" class="contents-chevron size-4 shrink-0 transition-transform" />
		</summary>
		<nav aria-label="Sections">
			<ul class="mt-2 grid">
				{
					contents.map((item) => (
						<li>
							<a
								href={`#${item.id}`}
								class="group border-neutral/10 focus-visible:outline-accent flex min-h-11 items-center justify-between gap-4 border-b py-2 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-[-2px]">
								<span class="cubby-section-label font-semibold">{item.label}</span>
								<span class="text-base-content/55 text-sm">{item.note}</span>
							</a>
						</li>
					))
				}
			</ul>
		</nav>
	</details>
	<nav data-case-study-section-nav class="mx-auto hidden max-w-5xl px-5 pb-14 sm:block" aria-label="Sections">
		<p class="cubby-accent text-xs tracking-wide">Contents</p>
		<ul class="mt-4 grid gap-x-10 sm:grid-cols-2">
			{
				contents.map((item) => (
					<li>
						<a
							href={`#${item.id}`}
							class="group border-neutral/10 flex items-baseline justify-between gap-4 border-b py-3">
							<span class="cubby-section-label font-semibold">{item.label}</span>
							<span class="text-base-content/50 text-sm">{item.note}</span>
						</a>
					</li>
				))
			}
		</ul>
	</nav>

	<section id="notes" class="border-neutral/15 bg-base-100 scroll-mt-20 border-t">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-16">
			<div class="max-w-2xl">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold lg:text-3xl">What belongs on the dashboard</h2>
				<p class="text-base-content/85 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
					I want immediate access to the actions I use every day, especially logging tasks and limits. I need key
					information, like today's pickup time, right there too. I accepted a slightly denser dashboard to keep those
					within reach. A few sparse cards looks nicer, but that punishes me, I'd need many more clicks.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="mx-auto mt-10 grid max-w-4xl items-start gap-10 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-8">
				<figure class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[280px]">
					<Image
						src={dashboard}
						alt="Cubby home screen"
						width={280}
						densities={[1, 2, 3]}
						loading="lazy"
						decoding="async"
						class="border-neutral/20 block h-auto w-full border-2 shadow-sm"
					/>
					<figcaption class="text-base-content/55 mt-3 text-center text-sm leading-relaxed">
						Pinned routines and limits put what needs doing first.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[280px]">
					<Image
						src={dashboardRoutines}
						alt="Cubby home screen showing health, routines, and finance"
						width={280}
						densities={[1, 2, 3]}
						loading="lazy"
						decoding="async"
						class="border-neutral/20 block h-auto w-full border-2 shadow-sm"
					/>
					<figcaption class="text-base-content/55 mt-3 text-center text-sm leading-relaxed">
						Health, schedules, and finances surface when they need attention.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
				<figure class="mx-auto w-full max-w-[280px]">
					<Image
						src={dashboardFamily}
						alt="Cubby home screen showing family moments, meals, and travel"
						width={280}
						densities={[1, 2, 3]}
						loading="lazy"
						decoding="async"
						class="border-neutral/20 block h-auto w-full border-2 shadow-sm"
					/>
					<figcaption class="text-base-content/55 mt-3 text-center text-sm leading-relaxed">
						Family moments, meals, and travel stay together in the same scroll.
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<main class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5">
		{
			groups.map((group) => (
				<section id={group.label.toLowerCase()} class="scroll-mt-20 pt-14">
					<div class="border-neutral/20 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-3 border-b pb-3">
						<h2 class="text-base-content/85 text-sm font-semibold tracking-[0.18em] uppercase">{group.label}</h2>
						<span class="text-base-content/50 text-xs">{group.note}</span>
					</div>
					<p class="text-base-content/80 mt-5 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">{group.intro}</p>
					<div>
						{group.items.map((item) => (
							<article class="border-neutral/10 grid gap-8 border-b py-10 last:border-b-0 sm:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto] sm:gap-12">
								<div>
									<h3 class="text-2xl leading-snug font-bold lg:text-3xl">{item.headline}</h3>
									<div class="text-base-content/85 mt-5 grid gap-4 leading-relaxed">
										{item.paragraphs.map((paragraph) => (
											<p>{paragraph}</p>
										))}
									</div>
								</div>
								<div class="sm:w-[230px]">
									<ScreenshotSpecimen src={item.src} alt={item.alt} max="max-w-[230px]" />
								</div>
							</article>
						))}
					</div>
				</section>
			))
		}
	</main>

	<section id="recap" class="mx-auto max-w-5xl scroll-mt-20 px-5 py-16">
		<div class="grid items-start gap-10 sm:grid-cols-[auto_1fr] sm:gap-12">
			<ScreenshotSpecimen src={journalRecap} alt="Cubby journal recap" max="max-w-[230px]" />
			<div class="sm:pt-2">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold lg:text-3xl">Looking back at the week</h2>
				<p class="text-base-content/85 mt-4 max-w-md leading-relaxed">
					The separate trackers feed a weekly and monthly recap. Meals, workouts, and family moments can be read
					together, which gives us a reason to come back to what we logged.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<CubbyStory section="timeline" />

	<section id="lessons" class="border-neutral/15 bg-base-100 scroll-mt-20 border-t">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-16">
			<div class="max-w-2xl">
				<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold lg:text-3xl">Keeping it usable is the hard part</h2>
				<p class="text-base-content/85 mt-5 leading-relaxed">
					Each feature started with something I wanted to fix, so there was always a reason to add it. Together, they
					make for a lot of app. Keeping it easy to use is something I'm still struggling with.
				</p>
				<p class="text-base-content/85 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
					I liked the floating dock when I built it, but I kept misclicking it in actual use. Eventually I went back to
					a conventional navigation bar with labels, which worked much better.
				</p>
				<p class="text-base-content/85 mt-4 leading-relaxed">
					Building with agents makes it easy to add another feature. I still have to use it, notice what gets in the
					way, and decide what to simplify. Sometimes that means giving up an interaction I liked building.
				</p>
			</div>
			<details class="border-neutral/20 mt-10 max-w-3xl border-t pt-5">
				<summary
					class="cubby-accent cursor-pointer py-2 font-semibold focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-4"
					>Development notes</summary
				>
				<div class="text-base-content/85 mt-5 grid gap-6 leading-relaxed">
					<p>
						GPT-5.6 Sol was quick to push out changes and happy to layer more onto the frontend architecture. I only
						noticed how much had accumulated when I stopped to audit it. Some pages loaded things they didn't need, and
						removing the obvious waste cut important startup costs by around 40-50%.
					</p>
					<p>
						That was enough of an improvement for me. I left the slowest few percent of load times alone after dealing
						with the large sources of waste.
					</p>
					<p>
						Moving the static frontend to Cloudflare Workers freed the VPS to run the services that needed a server, and
						the free tier covered the frontend.
					</p>
				</div>
			</details>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="border-neutral/15 bg-base-100 border-t">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-12">
			<div class="mt-10">
				<p class="text-base-content/55 text-xs tracking-wide">Built with</p>
				<dl class="mt-4 max-w-md">
					{
						stack.map((row) => (
							<div class="border-neutral/10 grid grid-cols-[8rem_1fr] gap-4 border-b py-2 last:border-b-0">
								<dt class="text-base-content/55 text-[0.7rem] font-semibold tracking-wider uppercase">{row.role}</dt>
								<dd class="text-base-content/85 text-sm">{row.tools}</dd>
							</div>
						))
					}
				</dl>
				<p class="text-base-content/55 mt-4 max-w-md text-sm leading-relaxed">
					Sentry used to be in here too, but it slowed builds more than it was worth, so it came out. A self-hosted
					GitHub Actions runner checks the web app, main API and retained Roamichi service before changes ship.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</div>

<style>
	.cubby-case-study {
		--color-base-100: #faf8f2;
		--color-base-200: #f5f2e9;
		--color-base-content: #25382c;
		--cubby-accent: #365c43;
	}

	.cubby-accent,
	.cubby-visit {
		color: var(--cubby-accent);
	}

	.cubby-visit {
		border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--cubby-accent) 45%, transparent);
		outline-color: var(--cubby-accent);
	}

	.cubby-contents-summary {
		border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--cubby-accent) 25%, transparent);
	}

	.cubby-section-label {
		transition: color var(--motion-duration-short) var(--motion-ease-out);
	}

	[data-case-study-section-nav] a:hover .cubby-section-label,
	[data-case-study-section-nav] a:focus-visible .cubby-section-label,
	[data-case-study-section-nav] a[aria-current='location'] .cubby-section-label {
		color: var(--cubby-accent);
	}

	[data-case-study-section-nav] a[aria-current='location'] .cubby-section-label {
		text-decoration: underline;
		text-decoration-color: var(--cubby-accent);
		text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
		text-underline-offset: 0.25rem;
	}

	.case-study-contents summary::-webkit-details-marker {
		display: none;
	}

	.case-study-contents[open] .contents-chevron {
		transform: rotate(90deg);
	}
</style>
